WEBVTT
TITLE Er Model — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Section 9 of the GATE 2027 syllabus opens with two words: ER-model.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:14.000
Three primitives: an entity set, its attributes, and a relationship set.

3
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:21.000
Each primitive has a shape, and each shape has a mapping rule.

4
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Design first, tables second: every count in this lesson is computed, not remembered.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.000
An entity set is a set, so its members are distinguishable — that needs a key.

6
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:44.000
A simple attribute is one column. A composite attribute is flattened into several.

7
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:51.000
A multivalued attribute is the one that forces a table of its own.

8
00:00:51.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Seven phone values for four readers: three in one cell if flattened, one per row if split.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.000
A relationship set associates entities, and it has a degree: two here, three when three sets join.

10
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:14.000
An attribute of the relationship belongs to the pair, not to either entity.

11
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:21.000
A relationship attribute is what forces the third table in a many-to-many design.

12
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Borrows(rid, isbn, copy_no, since), keyed by all three.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Cardinality ratio says how many partners one entity may have.

14
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Count them: 3 by 2 gives 64 many-to-many instances, 13 one-to-one, and 256 for 3 by 4 one-to-many.

15
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:51.000
The model enumerates every subset and compares it with the closed form.

16
00:01:51.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Same diagram, same sizes, different constraint: 27 instances become 8.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.000
A single line is partial participation; a double line is total.

18
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Total participation is what makes a merged foreign key NOT NULL.

19
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:21.000
Partial participation leaves nulls: two of the five readers never borrow.

20
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:30.000
GATE CSE 2018 Q11: many-to-one with both sides total means exactly one partner for the many side.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.000
A weak entity set has no key of its own: a double rectangle.

22
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:44.000
The partial key alone collides: six copies share two copy numbers.

23
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:51.000
The owner key completes it: (isbn, copy_no) is unique, six rows and six keys.

24
00:02:51.000 --> 00:03:00.000
GATE CSE 2024 Set 2: only the weak set must participate totally in the identifying relationship.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.000
Specialisation puts subclasses under a superclass: Student and Faculty under Person.

26
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:14.000
Strategy A: a superclass table and one table per subclass — three tables here.

27
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:21.000
Strategy B: one table with a type column — one table, many nulls.

28
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:30.000
Strategy C needs total and disjoint; with an overlap, three persons become four rows.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.000
The seven rules, in the order that avoids rework.

30
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:44.000
Four entities give four tables; the weak one takes a composite key.

31
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:51.000
The one-to-many and the one-to-one make no table: bid lands in Copy, rid in Branch.

32
00:03:51.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Six tables. Rejoining gives back all five borrow rows; folding Borrows into Reader loses two.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.000
GATE CSE 2015 Set 1 Q38: m:n between E1 and E2, 1:n between E1 and E3 — four relations.

34
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:14.000
GATE CSE 2008 Q82: two strong entities and one weak — three tables, and M gains P1.

35
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:21.000
GATE CSE 2005 Q15: one 1:N and one M:N between the same pair — three tables.

36
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:30.000
All three keys are reproduced by the model, not copied from an answer key.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.000
Step one: read the participation. Single line partial, double line total.

38
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:42.000
Step two: read the cardinality, and say which side is which.

39
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:48.000
Step three: look for a weak entity — double rectangle, partial key, owner.

40
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:54.000
Step four: look for multivalued attributes and for an ISA hierarchy.

41
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Step five: count and justify each table: strong entities plus weak plus M:N plus multivalued.

